{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1558071116,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MzMyNTQ1NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3316#issuecomment-493325455"},"message":"From my POV it has little benefit to implement this without something like https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/71 -- which we cannot do ATM.\n\nI also keep thinking why we actually make `create` and `install` perform superdataset commits. It is nice for scenarios that are like our tests: do one thing, double check that it actually happened, run git status again to be sure... But in any real-world, non-developer use that I am aware of datasets are assembled in various steps that are typically concluded with a final `save` call -- the more complicated test setups we have seem to confirm that (but it may also be that I wrote them ;-). So all those intermediate commits are somewhat useless and slow things down.\n\nSo if `save --amend` would come and further tweaks and features of the underlying `git commit` would be exposed, all those commands with just `--message` would be crippled in contrast. I think I'd prefer to have `save` be the only command to do handling of dataset tree operations, and leave others within the scope of a single dataset.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558100249,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MzQ1NjM0NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3316#issuecomment-493456345"},"message":"\u003e So if `save --amend` would come and further tweaks and features of the underlying `git commit` would be exposed, all those commands with just `--message` would be crippled in contrast.\n\nnot sure what/who gets crippled.  It is like `git commit` is crippled since there is `git commit --amend` to be.\n\n\u003e leave others within the scope of a single dataset.\n\nnot sure how it relates to the \"no auto-`save`\" besides that they already are, e.g. not calling save recursively if I get it right, but could cause a \"cascade\" of saves to save all the way to the super ds with `-S` (probably gone now) or `-d ^`.\n\nHaving said that, re the actual question of either to remove auto-saving -- need to think about it.\n\n**pros**: It would indeed eliminate some tension points (e.g. with the semantic of `install` with `-d` vs without #3230 mixing in difference in path handling and action) and possibly reduce complexity.\n\n**cons**: But in some cases auto-saving would not even be avoidable, e.g. any recursive operation on a hierarchy which would be altering subdatasets. E.g. `aggregate-metadata` (if storing into each subdataset as well), `update --merge` (merges).  Then `run` **must** save ;)    So the situation with `install` and `create` is probably different and I will no longer mention other ones.\n\nAlso it seems to go away a bit from some initial ideology of \"stay clean\", so datalad operations in general should leave the tree nicely saved etc.  That is why it became the default mode of operation and `--no-save` was introduced to mitigate in some cases.  But I rarely used `--no-save` in any recent times, but did rely on `-d` vs none from time to time to pretty much achieve \"no save\" for `install` or `create`.\n\n\u003e I also keep thinking why we actually make `create` and `install` perform superdataset commits\n\nIn general I do rely on `install -d . smth` and `create -d . subds`. But typically since I am crippled in subdirectories due to aforementioned mixed semantic.  But I am a \"developer\" so I guess not the target audience here? ;)  I would say it is useful beyond tests since also encourages a dedicated commit for a specific action, so then there is no pile up of changes stuffed into a single commit with a non-descriptive message.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558100249