mention other ones.\n\nAlso it seems to go away a bit from some initial ideology of \"stay clean\", so datalad operations in general should leave the tree nicely saved etc.  That is why it became the default mode of operation and `--no-save` was introduced to mitigate in some cases.  But I rarely used `--no-save` in any recent times, but did rely on `-d` vs none from time to time to pretty much achieve \"no save\" for `install` or `create`.  So I guess we are not enforcing that methodology anyways fully.\n\n\u003e I also keep thinking why we actually make `create` and `install` perform superdataset commits\n\nIn general I do rely on `install -d . smth` and `create -d . subds`. But typically since I am crippled in subdirectories due to aforementioned mixed semantic.  But I am a \"developer\" so I guess not the target audience here? ;)  I would say it is useful beyond tests since also encourages a dedicated commit for a specific action, so then there is no pile up of changes stuffed into a single commit with a non-descriptive message.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558100358,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIyMzgxNDQ5Mw=="},"target":"39d557457edc92612563fa3962fd104d4b9159120dbe27d9b43d249a697d67d4","message":"\u003e So if `save --amend` would come and further tweaks and features of the underlying `git commit` would be exposed, all those commands with just `--message` would be crippled in contrast.\n\nnot sure what/who gets crippled.  It is like `git commit` is crippled since there is `git commit --amend` to me.\n\n\u003e leave others within the scope of a single dataset.\n\nnot sure how it relates to the \"no auto-`save`\" besides that they already are, e.g. not calling save recursively if I get it right, but could cause a \"cascade\" of saves to save all the way to the super ds with `-S` (probably gone now) or `-d ^`.\n\nHaving said that, re the actual question of either to remove auto-saving -- need to think about it.\n\n**pros**: It would indeed eliminate some tension points (e.g. with the semantic of `install` with `-d` vs without #3230 mixing in difference in path handling and action) and possibly reduce complexity.\n\n**cons**: But in some cases auto-saving would not even be avoidable, e.g. any recursive operation on a hierarchy which would be altering subdatasets. E.g. `aggregate-metadata` (if storing into each subdataset as well), `update --merge` (merges).  Then `run` **must** save ;)    So the situation with `install` and `create` is probably different and I will no longer mention other ones.\n\nAlso it seems to go away a bit from some initial ideology of \"stay clean\", so datalad operations in general should leave the tree nicely saved etc.  That is why it became the default mode of operation and `--no-save` was introduced to mitigate in some cases.  But I rarely used `--no-save` in any recent times, but did rely on `-d` vs none from time to time to pretty much achieve \"no save\" for `install` or `create`.  So I guess we are not enforcing that methodology anyways fully.\n\n\u003e I also keep thinking why we actually make `create` and `install` perform superdataset commits\n\nIn general I do rely on `install -d . smth` and `create -d . subds`. But typically I am crippled in subdirectories due to aforementioned mixed semantic.   But I am a \"developer\" so I guess not the target audience here? ;)  I would say it is useful beyond tests since also encourages a dedicated commit for a specific action, so then there is no pile up of changes stuffed into a single commit with a non-descriptive message.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1558103697,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MzQ3NjM4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3316#issuecomment-493476385"},"message":"I think you talk a bit past each other.\nIf I get it right, @mih is suggesting, that `create -d . something` should simply not exist in the sense that it commits to `.`. Instead: `create something` + `save -d . -r`. So - no paradigm shift. Both commands are leaving the dataset clean. That is: The dataset they are operating on!\nRe crippled: That was an anticipation of consequences. IF we now let all those commands pass a message and then save itself becomes richer allowing for more git-commit-arguments, then naturally the wish will rise to pass them also into all commands, that are committing something and have a `-m`.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558123325,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MzU4MTQ5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3316#issuecomment-493581498"},"message":"\u003e `create -d . something` should simply not exist in the sense that it commits to `.`. Instead: `create something` + `save -d . -r`\n\nthat is exactly what is my understanding... but to be specific in the case of `create` it should become\n`create something \u0026\u0026 save -d . something` -- there should be no side-effect of causing saving of the entire tree with `-r`. So the cost is ok -- just duplicate entire initial command replacing `create` with `save`.\n\nIn case of install it would be more interesting since user AKA programmer would need to figure out where URL was installed in case of scripting to not rely on matching the behavior of datalad (not rocket science but it took us 16 lines of Python code for `_get_installationpath_from_url`).  In Python it should become smth like \n```python\nsubds = ds.install(url)\nds.save(subds.path)\n```\n\nIn shell I think it would be as lovely as\n```shell\nsubds=$(datalad -f '{path}' install -d \"$ds\" \"$url\")\ndatalad save -d \"$ds\" \"$subds\"\n```\nand fun will come whenever scripts are not coded with `set -e` to fail right away and user/developer relies on a single command exit status to do something like \n```shell\ndatalad install -d \"$ds\" \"$url\" \u0026\u0026 echo \"all is good\"`\n```\nit should then become\n```shell\nsubds=$(datalad -f '{path}' install -d \"$ds\" \"$url\")\n[ ! -z \"$subds\" ] \u0026\u0026 echo \"all is good\"\n```\nregular users of cause can type fast and know completion so they can avoid it by simply `ls` and copy/pasting or completing the name of the directory (hopefully that would be the one they just installed)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558123508,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MzU4MjM0OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3316#issuecomment-493582349"},"message":"\u003e Both commands are leaving the dataset clean. That is: The dataset they are operating on!\n\nin the \"two separate commands\" tandem, dataset **you are in** is not clean after the first one.  But that is not a \"shift\" really, since as I have mentioned those commands without `-d` already do that.  But it neither gets us closer to purity in that regard.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558124238,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MzU4NTcwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3316#issuecomment-493585700"},"message":"\u003e Re crippled: That was an anticipation of consequences. IF we now let all those commands pass a message and then save itself becomes richer allowing for more git-commit-arguments, then naturally the wish will rise to pass them also into all commands, that are committing something and have a `-m`.\n\ngotcha! But should we then stop saving in any other command which current does/need saving?  They all might need to make use of those options!\n\nATM, if I need something custom, I would rely on `--nosave` and then `save` or commit with those custom save/commit options.  I think having an API which is of assistance in \u003e90% of cases, while allowing for customization when needed, is convenient.  That is why, adding general ability to specify commit message to such commands actions of which would be saved (the original issue) would be convenient as well.  Forcing the split into two commands invocation complicating their use to save on a single command line option convenient in many (but not all, thus \"crippled\"), ... might indeed be more flexible but definitely not more convenient.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1558124238,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIyMzk4OTUzOQ=="},"target":"1423c7d8faa21b40b54e384681c2593499dbf590c9a0439714d5786afb69f8c2","message":"\u003e Re crippled: That was an anticipation of consequences. IF we now let all those commands pass a message and then save itself becomes richer allowing for more git-commit-arguments, then naturally the wish will rise to pass them also into all commands, that are committing something and have a `-m`.\n\ngotcha! But should we then stop saving in any other command which current does/need saving?  They all might need to make use of those options!\n\nATM, if I need something custom, I would rely on `--nosave` and then `save` or commit with those custom save/commit options.  I think having an API which is of assistance in \u003e90% of cases, while allowing for customization when needed, is convenient.  That is why, adding general ability to specify commit message to such commands actions of which would be saved (the original issue) would be convenient as well.  Forcing the split into two commands invocation complicating their use to save on a single command line option convenient in many (but not all, thus \"crippled\") cases, ... might indeed be more flexible but definitely not more convenient.","files":null}]}